# Benchmark Execution Policy

Apply this policy before requesting or launching any benchmark expected to run
longer than 15 minutes.

- Profile a representative 10–50-step run first. Record wall time, CPU/GPU
  utilization, RAM, solver calls and extrapolated full-run ETA.
- Verify that configured resources are actually used. Thread-pool creation is
  not evidence of parallel execution; report observed worker/thread usage.
- Run matched serial/parallel A/B workloads before claiming speedup. A
  microbenchmark does not establish end-to-end benchmark speedup.
- Make the accelerator decision explicit: explain why CPU, GPU or distributed
  execution fits the actual bottleneck.
- Separate correctness gates from performance gates. Passing mathematical
  parity does not imply acceptable runtime.
- Add resumable checkpoints and test resume before a long run. Limit expected
  lost work after interruption to at most 15 minutes where practical.
- Add heartbeat/progress output with completed work, elapsed time, current ETA,
  resource utilization diagnostics and cache/checkpoint status.
- Define stop conditions and a time/resource budget before full execution.
  Never recommend a multi-hour run if the pilot misses its efficiency gate.
- For multi-seed studies, evaluate sample-, seed- and process-level parallelism
  and avoid nested oversubscription.
- Record the pilot command, environment, measurements, rejected designs and
  final execution decision in the research log before requesting the full run.

Default sequence: correctness smoke → resource profile → matched A/B → resume
test → one-seed pilot → full registered benchmark.
